A||||11||skitime||3800 Flywheel balancing?||||5||
Z||000000||skitime||11-29-2001||06:17 PM||||Ok 3800SC people, help me out.  I am putting a 5 speed Getrag trans to the my 3800SC engine.  I have a 88 Fiero flywheel and I have a 3800 Camaro flywheel.  The Fiero flywheel would have to have the bolt holes filled and redrill from 6 holes to eight holes.  The 3800 Camaro will have to be machined to the thickness of the Fiero flywheel.  So far that much I know.  I felt the 3800 Camaro flywheel would be the better choice.  I know the 3800 Camaro flywheel will need to be rebalanced to match the 3800SC automatic flywheel. My question is the 3800SC an internally or externally balanced engine.  Besides the question, I would appreciate any words of wisdom you might share on flywheel/clutch issues that might be useful.  I already have a V8 Archie clutch to go in the setup.   Thanks<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-2.JPG"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Dgzfiero||11-29-2001||09:20 PM||||Ican try to call my mech and ask him for you tomorrow. If noone else answers this for you that is.  Mine is a 2000 year model I donn't know if there is a difference in years.||||reg||1
Z||000002||skitime||11-29-2001||09:21 PM||||Thanks DGZfiero I would appreciate it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||BOILERMAKER||11-29-2001||09:54 PM||||Going by the little knowledge I know, I have come to this conclusion.<P>When I bored the 2.8 to a internally balanced 3.2 I had to have a neutrally balanced flywheel.  When I put the 3800 together, I had to have a balanced flywheel, so I would assume that the 3800 is externally balanced due to the flywheel having to be balanced.<P>The camaro flywheel has to be shaved down to allow clearance for the transmission.  It has to be taken down to a minimum level (what level that is, I don't know) according to Chris West so that the pressure plate won't start interfering with the walls of the transmission.  I would call him because he is a very wise person when it comes to this.  He also sells these flywheels in case you wondered.<P>That's about all I know.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Dgzfiero||11-30-2001||07:48 PM|||| I talked to Tom earlier and he said it is an internally balanced engine. He also said you'd want a nuetrally balanced flywheel. As far as the flywheel/clutch stuff the Archie stuff will work good. <BR> Doug||||reg||1
Z||000005||BOILERMAKER||11-30-2001||11:58 PM||||I'm absolutely POSITIVE my '99 3800SC engine does NOT take a neutrally balanced flywheel.||||reg||1
Z||000006||skitime||12-01-2001||08:53 AM||||The original automatic flywheel has a weight one side and holes holes on the opposite.  The 3800 Camaro flywheel has a balancing band on the backside that only goes 3/4 the way around. These do not seem like flywheels for an internally balanced engine.  I am getting confused as who is right. I got a email response from a nice forum member who said- <P>"I work in the plant that builds the 3800SC engine. I have been with the engine since it was first concieved. All 3800's are externally balanced by means of a weight on the flywheel and drilled balance holes and by a slug on the inside of the harmonic balancer and again drilled balance holes."  <P>Seems he should know and is correct since that describes the flywheel that was on my engine but who is correct?  Is everyone balancing these engines incorrectly?  Inquiring minds want to know.  Thanks ||||reg||1
Z||000007||skitime||12-01-2001||09:21 AM||||PS... I was told by others that I had to use a 88 Fiero flywheel which is a neutrally balanced flywheel.<P>One more bit of information from the guy that works at the 3800SC plant. He said--<P>The Pontiac division is asking for a new variant of the 3800 S/C to produce at least 300 hp. As of right now the plan includes a steel crank (present crank is nodular iron) powdered metal rods and significantly larger supercharger. It's not firm but Pontiac is really pushing for it. It will go in a limited production Bonneville. <P><p>[This message has been edited by skitime (edited 12-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||rockcrawl||12-01-2001||12:02 PM||||The 3800 SII is externally ballanced. The new flywheel should be ballanced to match the flexplate that came off the engine. An incorrect amount of wieght, or a weight at the wrong spot will cause serious vibration, so if you can't find  someone to match-ballance it, I suggest using a neutrally ballanced wheel. For some reason I think the supercharged engine is ballanced differently than the n/a, so the Camaro flywheel can't just be turned down to the correct thickness, it also needs to be re-ballanced.||||reg||1
Z||000009||jimmybpei||12-01-2001||09:15 PM||||now that i think of it i think that my flywheel should ahve been ground. Just by the way that went in and how its engaging.<P>Should have checked the thicknesses of both and the thickness of the crank as opposed to the 2.8 cranck. <P>oih well it works so " if it aint broke don't fix it" <P>just a few extra thing for you to look at SKITIME from my own experience. will know better for the 4.9 or maybe pick your brain for a 3800 sc, only when you get yours finished. Hope its done by april  when i get there.<P>Good luck and have fun<BR>Later<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jimmybpeisig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Wannabe||12-03-2001||09:20 AM||||Why not pick up an aluminum flywheel from Racetech in Toronto. $550CDN ready to bolt on.||||reg||1
Z||000011||BOILERMAKER||12-03-2001||11:03 AM||||Sure it's ready to bolt on, but when you get the vibrations because of no balancing, don't come crying to me.  I've got a buddy that had that canadian company do his 3800SC conversion and they BALANCED THE PRESSURE PLATE - so his mechanic says -  I haven't seen it in person.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">  <P>When he gathers up the funds, he's switching back to a regular flywheel and throwing that whole setup in the crapper.||||reg||1
